{
  "kind": "tea",
  "teaId": "hagar-seen",
  "text": "A pregnant slave girl runs into a desert to die, and God shows up and lets her name him.\n\nLet's set it. Sarai can't have children. So she gives her Egyptian handmaid Hagar to Abram, to get a child through her. Nobody asks Hagar anything. She is a solution to somebody else's problem.\n\nShe conceives. Tension explodes. Sarai says to Abram, \"My wrong be upon thee.\" And Abram, husband of the year, goes, \"Behold, thy maid is in thy hand. Do to her as it pleaseth thee.\"\n\nHe hands her back. And the text says Sarai dealt hardly with her.\n\nSo Hagar runs.\n\nPregnant. Alone. Into wilderness. With no plan, because there isn't one.\n\nAnd the angel of the Lord finds her by a spring of water. Not in a temple. Not in a camp. By a spring, in the middle of nowhere.\n\nAnd he says, \"Hagar, Sarai's maid, whence camest thou? And whither wilt thou go?\"\n\nWhere did you come from. Where are you going.\n\nShe answers the first one. \"I flee from the face of my mistress Sarai.\" She does not answer the second, because she has nowhere to go and they both know it.\n\nThen he tells her she'll bear a son, and to call him Ishmael, because the Lord hath heard thy affliction.\n\nAnd then Hagar does something nobody in Scripture has done yet.\n\nShe names God. \"Thou God seest me.\"\n\nA runaway. A foreigner. A slave. And she is the one who gets to say it.",
  "voice": "FGY2WhTYpPnrIDTdsKH5",
